a big topic for us in 2019 so far has  been the growing threat from the  anti-vaccine community not just in the  United States but in a lot of different  parts of the world mentioned early on in  the year that anti-vaccine sentiment has  been listed by the World Health  Organization as one of the top 10 global  health threats of the year  Canada is getting really serious about  this issue and I absolutely love it  they've sent out 6,000 suspension  notices to parents of children in the  Waterloo Region which is in the province  of Ontario and the notices say that the  school department doesn't have proof of  vaccinations or valid exemption requests  for these roughly 6100 kids now this is  not all anti vaccine sentiment and I'll  explain what's going on with a lot of  these kids it's just a matter of getting  the vaccination records to the public  health officials with some of these kids  they are not vaccinated and the parents  are going to have to make a decision  about whether they're going to have  their kids vaccinated by March 26th  whether they are going to pull them out  of public school and send them to  private school if that's something that  they can afford and are interested in  doing if they're going to request a an  exemption from vaccinations or if the  kids are going to end up suspended  potentially for 20 days from school and  there's a really interesting dynamic  here because a lot of people emailed me  and then they said hope David I'm  reading this story but schools have  required vaccination records in Canada  for a really long time why is this  happening all of a sudden and it's  really interesting a lot of the school  districts that require vaccination  records didn't really pursue the records  because everybody was vaccinated or  almost everybody was vaccinated there  was no problem with the anti-vaccine  movement the anti-vaccine movement is  now growing so nothing has changed in  terms of requiring vaccination records  what's changed is that now the schools  are getting stricter about actually  requesting those records and what what's  also important just to remind everybody  about here is that vaccinations are  not mandatory in the sense that the  government is forcing people to  vaccinate themselves or their children  Canada isn't forcing anybody to get a  vaccine Canada or better said Waterloo  Ontario is saying if you want your kids  to go to public school you either need  to give us a vaccination record or a  valid waiver from vaccinations it's all  up to the parents the parents can say  I'm in a home school the parents can say  I'm going to send them to a private  school the parents can say I'm going to  try to get an exemption from  vaccinations and parents who have  vaccinated their kids should be really  glad that this is being done because you  want a system that's going to shake out  the anti-vaxxers who might be  endangering their kids of course no  vaccine is 100% effective herd immunity  is the name of the game you have kids  who are immunocompromised who can't be  vaccinated you have kids who are doing  the right thing and getting vaccinated  their parents are doing the right thing  but because the vaccines not a hundred  percent everybody who opts merely not to  vaccinate because they don't want to is  putting everybody at risk and what you  really need to understand here is that  there are centuries of medical progress  with vaccines as one of the greatest  contributors to that progress that  progress is being threatened by people  who just don't want to vaccinate their  kids religious exemptions should not be  allowed period send your kid to a  private school homeschool them don't use  religion as a tool to endanger a  publicly funded government-run school  system it just can't be allowed to  happen and meanwhile it's not just in  Canada that this is a problem the  anti-vaccine movement is exploding I  have so many stories I've been hoping to  get to about this we learned about a  father whose family is the epicenter of  the vancouver canada measles outbreak  his name is emmanuel Bilodeau he says  the reason he didn't vaccinate his kids  is he was afraid that the measles  vaccine the MMR vaccine would give them  autism Japan is having a measles  outbreak there's a religious group  called Q sankyo which operates in the me  a  Prefecture that's the center of a  measles outbreak they are now  apologizing for the outbreak they are  known to spread anti vaccine nonsense  they're saying they're not going to do  it anymore  and then by the way Madagascar is now  dealing with almost 70,000 reported  cases of measles and the connection to  the anti vaccine sentiment in Madagascar  is still being explored we don't know to  what extent that's connected so it's  getting really bad parts of the United  States Washington State and Oregon are  epicenters in the US Canada Australia  we've talked about a number of countries  in Europe including Italy and one other  that I'm forgetting Japan now dealing  with this and Madagascar now dealing  with this and there are people who sort  of laugh at this and say David it's  Darwinian selection anybody who doesn't  get vaccinated is going to be less  likely to live long enough to pass on  their genes to children and you'll just  sort of have anti-vaxxers die off not  true because unfortunately much of the  risk around not vaccinating people is  around people who can't get vaccines the  vaccines are not a hundred percent  effective so it's not just Darwinian  selection that we can all laugh at this  is a serious health issue and that's why  the w-h-o has designated it as such for  2019
 

